what is the diff between powershell and powershell 7 in start menu win11
In the Windows 11 Start menu, PowerShell and PowerShell 7 point to two completely different engines built on different underlying software frameworks:
Feature	PowerShell (Windows PowerShell)	PowerShell 7
Executable Name	powershell.exe	pwsh.exe
Framework	Built on legacy .NET Framework (v4.x)	Built on modern, cross-platform .NET Core
Version	Stuck at v5.1 (No new feature updates)	v7.x (Actively developed and updated)
OS Compatibility	Windows-only	Cross-platform (Windows, macOS, Linux)
Performance	Slower execution & startup time	Up to 3–5x faster multi-threading (ForEach-Object -Parallel)
Default In Win11	Pre-installed out of the box	Installed separately via Winget, Store, or MSI
Target Path	C:\Windows\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\	C:\Program Files\PowerShell\7\
Key Functional Differences
ForEach-Object -Parallel: PowerShell 7 natively supports parallel execution to run loops concurrently across multiple CPU threads. Windows PowerShell 5.1 runs loops sequentially (single-threaded).
Modern Syntax Operators: PowerShell 7 introduces pipeline operators like the Ternary operator (a ? b : c), Null-coalescing (??), and Null-assignment (??=).
Error Views: PowerShell 7 defaults to a simplified ConciseView that highlights the line and exact reason for a script error rather than flooding the console with multi-line red call stacks.
Module Compatibility: While PowerShell 7 can load legacy Windows modules via an inline compatibility layer, a tiny subset of older WMI or COM-dependent Windows modules only function natively inside Windows PowerShell 5.1.
Unless you are running a legacy enterprise script built specifically for .NET Framework 4.x, PowerShell 7 (pwsh) is the recommended default for all scripting and command-line work.
